WEBVTT

NOTE automatic transcription, uncorrected

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.610
I didn't really know what to make of him. He was, in those days, young, slim,

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:15.814
immensely energetic, and very, very knowledgeable on architectural history, which I

00:00:15.814 --> 00:00:17.000
appreciated.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:25.182
But the general impression was that there were some very interesting things to be

00:00:25.182 --> 00:00:27.000
done in the office

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.000
and I would like to be part of them.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.000
The job I got, which was with the British Rail Chief Architects Department,

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:40.647
I took the job simply to get to London basically, as a lot of people did at that

00:00:40.647 --> 00:00:41.000
time.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:47.300
and so when he made me the offer, it was one I couldn't refuse.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:50.480
It was at one of our meetings and he said,

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:52.680
well, would you like to join us?

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:58.580
I can't offer you a very high salary,

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:01.000
but that goes without saying, Cedric.

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:04.040
And boy, it was not a high salary.

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:09.080
£1,500 a year in those days.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:10.360
£63, remember.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:13.572
Now that was what I was getting at the time, so effectively he said, I'll match your

00:01:13.572 --> 00:01:13.840
salary.

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:16.860
Well, I was the one and only assistant.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:23.106
He had a secretary, Dick Baudelaar, who was doing the technical work on the farm

00:01:23.106 --> 00:01:23.600
palace,

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:29.440
the cybernetics work there, was in part of the time,

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:34.040
but I was the only quote-unquote architect besides Cedric.

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:36.560
It was immensely stimulating.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:40.560
He was in and out of the office all the time.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.560
We got in very early and as we progressed on to things,

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:48.560
our way of working was to chat things over when he was there.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:52.560
Then he would leave drawing, drawings after drawing after drawing

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:56.560
and say, I think this should do that and I think this should go like this.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:00.560
Particularly on the Potteries Thinkbelt.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:04.560
It was a mixture of graphic and

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:12.069
verbal communication basically. We started this, it was, Cedric had had it in his

00:02:12.069 --> 00:02:12.440
mind

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:20.998
I think for a long time and when we ceased work on the Fun Palace, the main project

00:02:20.998 --> 00:02:21.720
because

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:31.919
it was being stalled by the GLC, he launched into that and what we did first of all,

00:02:31.919 --> 00:02:32.040
I

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:37.880
I mean the overall concept was explained as it were, chatted through.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:43.746
But we then got large scale maps of the whole area, which is quite a lot of maps

00:02:43.746 --> 00:02:45.820
because it's quite a large area.

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:55.860
And we sorted out the main constituents of the plan on those ordnance survey maps.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:59.440
Because we had the contours, we had the buildings.

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:04.060
we had practically all the information you needed. I did know the pottery

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:09.640
slightly because a friend of mine had gone to work as a director of the

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:18.060
Victoria Theatre in Stoke-on-Trent so I knew it vaguely but we worked out the

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:23.820
detailed implementation. The basic principle is the triangle, right?

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:28.760
You've got the three transfer areas, Maidley, Pittshill and Myrhor.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:35.394
And then what was interesting was to sort out all the usable railway lines, of which

00:03:35.394 --> 00:03:36.520
there were many.

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:38.540
There were a hell of a lot of sidings.

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:42.160
So these became the faculty sidings.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:47.420
And then the housing, there was an awful lot of waste land.

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:53.587
land. So we devised four different types of housing to cope with different

00:03:53.587 --> 00:03:54.420
locations,

00:03:54.420 --> 00:04:01.173
different soil conditions. I mean the whole of the potteries at that time was just

00:04:01.173 --> 00:04:01.420
one

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:08.179
vast excavation. There were the mines, coal mines, there was the pottery clay mines,

00:04:08.179 --> 00:04:08.420
the

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:15.022
rest. The whole thing had been turned over, gouged out and over about 200 years. So

00:04:15.022 --> 00:04:15.420
there

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:19.020
So there was an awful lot of interesting topography.

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:24.540
There were the spoil heaps, the excavations,

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:28.800
and there was an awful lot of just wasteland in between

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:31.600
where the industry had been and then gone.

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:37.680
So we chose our housing sites, as I said, from the Ordnance Survey maps,

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:41.160
and it wasn't until the thing was pretty well settled,

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:45.060
in my mind at least, that we went up there.

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:48.420
and photograph the sites which we selected.

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:55.173
And what was very interesting was that it was pretty well as I'd imagined it would

00:04:55.173 --> 00:04:55.420
be,

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:59.420
because I knew the thing now in three dimensions, you know, from the maps.

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:09.400
So it was a dreadful, drizzly grey day, so that's why the photographs are dreadfully

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:11.420
grey and drizzly,

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:16.660
be in, which is no bad thing because they don't glamorise the site at all. The site

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:21.308
was not glamorous. It was a mucky old place then. It still is to some extent now,

00:05:21.308 --> 00:05:21.480
but

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:33.050
boy was it mucky then. So we toured the whole area. We went to the transfer areas.

00:05:33.050 --> 00:05:34.320
We looked

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:40.395
at the housing sites. There was nothing really that needed changing. So it was an

00:05:40.395 --> 00:05:41.220
interesting

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:48.003
experiment in what you can do with just visual information which I later used as

00:05:48.003 --> 00:05:49.020
training for

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:53.224
my own students when I was teaching at the AA. It's a very good discipline in fact.

00:05:53.224 --> 00:05:53.680
How close

00:05:53.680 --> 00:06:01.069
to realisation? Nowhere near close to realisation because it was very much you could

00:06:01.069 --> 00:06:02.300
do this if you

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:08.650
had x million pounds you know. It was a demonstration thing it was not to be sold. I

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:09.860
mean we produced

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:20.075
these packages of drawings, the aerial photograph and everything, it was more a,

00:06:20.075 --> 00:06:21.480
well it was

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:31.553
a response to a remark, yeah, at that time the, I think it was Lord Kent who was

00:06:31.553 --> 00:06:32.560
Minister

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:40.772
in the education department and he got into a conversation and which developed into

00:06:40.772 --> 00:06:41.860
an argument

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:48.031
with Cedric at some occasion talking about universities and you know Cedric said

00:06:48.031 --> 00:06:48.340
look

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:53.043
you're going up the wrong path you shouldn't be doing so so Kenneth in exasperation

00:06:53.043 --> 00:06:53.780
said well why

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:59.169
don't you do something and show me so he said well I will yeah but it has as I say

00:06:59.169 --> 00:07:00.220
been in his mind

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:07.070
for some time. And this was therefore a demonstration of what you could do using

00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:09.040
existing infrastructure

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:16.640
and a new radical way of looking at learning, looking at education as a whole life

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:17.660
experience.

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:22.676
Remember at that time, if you went to university, whether it was the new

00:07:22.676 --> 00:07:24.000
universities or the

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:34.247
old ones, you stayed in student accommodation. This was Cedric's view, quite

00:07:34.247 --> 00:07:36.000
rightly, that

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:41.409
housing could benefit, new housing could benefit everybody in the area and students

00:07:41.409 --> 00:07:41.800
should

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:47.280
be used as a means of improving the housing stock and lifting it up and improving

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:48.160
expectations.

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:52.490
So that was a pretty radical idea at the time. It isn't now, of course, but it was

00:07:52.490 --> 00:07:52.720
then.

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:59.245
The second thing was that education could be in its own right an economic driver for

00:07:59.245 --> 00:08:02.740
a depressed area where the industry had gone.

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:06.860
And that of course is again today taken as granted.

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:13.863
If you want to push an area up you put a university in it because it's a powerhouse

00:08:13.863 --> 00:08:16.520
for high technology and so forth.

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:24.094
So that was, the two things were to normalise learning, in other words, not an ivory

00:08:24.094 --> 00:08:27.520
tower, but just part of everyday life.

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:32.020
Students are no different from anybody else, and everybody is a student at some time

00:08:32.020 --> 00:08:33.520
or other through their life.

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:40.861
And that, together with bringing the area up by the injection of technological

00:08:40.861 --> 00:08:41.520
change.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:43.080
So those were the two linchpins.

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:46.300
Also, I think there was a degree of romanticism there.

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:49.000
The idea of using the railway lines,

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:51.660
which was immense fun to work out,

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:55.260
was an interesting comment

00:08:55.260 --> 00:09:01.020
on how you could reuse existing infrastructure,

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:02.980
existing plant, existing kit,

00:09:03.580 --> 00:09:06.180
and not junk it, basically.

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:08.120
So to a certain extent,

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:10.540
that sort of echoed Bucky's view of recycling.

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:22.510
The housing thing also was, it led on to various housing studies, particularly the

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:24.520
sprawl housing,

00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:34.520
looking at a 25 year life housing, very low, very light footprint on the ground.

00:09:34.520 --> 00:09:39.520
So that developed later on into the various housing studies we did.

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:49.352
But the four types of housing were effectively to, you know, we said this is nothing

00:09:49.352 --> 00:09:49.820
new.

00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:54.120
People have looked at totally enclosed, totally controlled environments.

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:56.180
People have looked at suburban housing.

00:09:56.180 --> 00:10:04.120
People have looked at housing where you could slot in new units as required.

00:10:04.120 --> 00:10:06.320
There was nothing new about that.

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:16.900
So the three, three, four, four, three types, sprawl, battery and crate.

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:17.700
That's right.

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:18.680
So I got it wrong.

00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:19.560
It's not four, it's three.

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:19.840
Yeah.

00:10:20.140 --> 00:10:24.651
But we chose the word sprawl because we thought it would really get up the noses of

00:10:24.651 --> 00:10:25.140
the RIBA.

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:29.700
So there was a degree of bravado in there.

00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:39.277
But it was at no time, unlike the Fun Palace, at no time did we say, you know, when

00:10:39.277 --> 00:10:41.700
do we start building.

00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:48.522
We costed it of course. It could have been built, of course, but it was a

00:10:48.522 --> 00:10:51.700
demonstration project rather than,

00:10:51.700 --> 00:10:56.400
will somebody please lend me 10 million pounds, you know,

00:10:56.480 --> 00:10:58.140
whatever it would cost at the time.

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:02.720
And don't forget, we were, at the same time,

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:03.900
we were doing other projects.

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:06.040
I mean, it was fitting in with various other things.

00:11:06.040 --> 00:11:11.860
The London Heathrow Airport Northside study,

00:11:12.540 --> 00:11:17.660
later the Docksport building, the computer building,

00:11:17.660 --> 00:11:22.300
and the Fun Palace pilot project,

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:25.080
which did look as if it had a chance of getting off the ground.

00:11:25.080 --> 00:11:28.220
That was taken to quite a detailed degree.

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:33.620
So the thing was not a sit down and do it like that.

00:11:33.820 --> 00:11:37.120
It was in the cracks, which was a nice way of working, of course,

00:11:37.220 --> 00:11:41.140
because if you'd spent the day, as I sometimes did,

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:45.340
working out details of the tiling in the lavatory,

00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:50.900
It was quite nice to go up the scale like that to one of the transfer areas.

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:56.400
So it was a nice mix basically, as was everything in Cedric's office.

00:11:56.980 --> 00:12:07.249
It was always a very fruitful mix of people, ideas, the odd drink and so on and so

00:12:07.249 --> 00:12:08.000
forth.

00:12:08.120 --> 00:12:09.740
Very relaxed way of working.

00:12:09.740 --> 00:12:14.540
But actually, I don't think I've ever worked so hard in my life as I did in that

00:12:14.540 --> 00:12:14.960
office.

00:12:14.960 --> 00:12:18.820
but that's obviously part of the magic of the place,

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:21.120
that you didn't want to stop working, basically.

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:25.940
Typical day, Alfred Place, which we moved to shortly after George Street.

00:12:27.020 --> 00:12:33.980
And by that time, I got four rooms in Windmill Street,

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:35.740
which is just across Tottenham Court Road,

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:39.100
which I was converting to a flat with an actual bathroom,

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:41.220
which is the first one I'd had since I'd come to London.

00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:47.220
So it took me three minutes to walk to the office, which was very pleasant.

00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:52.220
Or indeed three minutes to walk home, which is more to the point.

00:12:52.220 --> 00:12:56.220
So I would get in about nine o'clock, something like that.

00:12:56.220 --> 00:13:00.220
Cedric would probably have been there since six.

00:13:00.220 --> 00:13:07.501
He would have left a lot of drawings and things if he was not going to be in that

00:13:07.501 --> 00:13:08.220
morning,

00:13:08.220 --> 00:13:14.871
morning which is quite frequently the case or then we would go over what I was going

00:13:14.871 --> 00:13:15.980
to do that day

00:13:15.980 --> 00:13:22.242
or how I thought we should proceed on something and so forth. But he had so many

00:13:22.242 --> 00:13:23.260
people coming

00:13:23.260 --> 00:13:29.234
to see him that it was not a continuous thing. He was in and out of his little

00:13:29.234 --> 00:13:30.460
cubbyhole office

00:13:30.460 --> 00:13:35.639
there all the time and he would shut himself away when he was thinking which was a

00:13:35.639 --> 00:13:36.460
very sensible

00:13:36.460 --> 00:13:48.000
thing to do. So then we might well have a hot brandy before lunch and I would go out

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:55.907
either back to the flat or get a sandwich or something like that and work through.

00:13:55.907 --> 00:13:56.100
It

00:13:56.100 --> 00:14:00.196
was, you know, there was no particular pattern to any day, it depends what he was

00:14:00.196 --> 00:14:00.600
doing at

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:06.297
the time or he might be abroad for example but there was a very close liaison on

00:14:06.297 --> 00:14:07.080
anything we

00:14:07.080 --> 00:14:12.787
were working i i mentioned tiles and lavatories when he was in the states at one

00:14:12.787 --> 00:14:14.000
point i was doing

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:20.770
the layout for the uh the toilets lavatories call you what you want for the docks

00:14:20.770 --> 00:14:21.940
board building

00:14:21.940 --> 00:14:28.817
which was quite an interesting problem in its own right because i i enjoy drainage

00:14:28.817 --> 00:14:29.740
like george

00:14:29.740 --> 00:14:37.915
at Edmund Street who said, I'm a drains man and stench pipes are my speciality. I

00:14:37.915 --> 00:14:38.420
enjoy

00:14:38.420 --> 00:14:44.363
designing plumbing. It's never been the same since cast iron and copper went. It's

00:14:44.363 --> 00:14:44.580
all

00:14:44.580 --> 00:14:52.171
plastic now. There's no interest in designing plumbing any longer. Anyway, so he

00:14:52.171 --> 00:14:52.740
sent a

00:14:52.740 --> 00:15:01.100
message saying, can you send the drawings of the lavatories by email, by airmail to

00:15:01.100 --> 00:15:09.190
me, which he did, you know, because he was, you know, it was a very close

00:15:09.190 --> 00:15:10.520
relationship

00:15:10.520 --> 00:15:21.275
between us in terms of detailing. Cedric had very interesting ideas on additive

00:15:21.275 --> 00:15:22.500
detailing

00:15:22.500 --> 00:15:24.900
which I picked up from him, basically,

00:15:25.520 --> 00:15:29.800
because they were a sort of loose-fit approach to detailing.

00:15:30.380 --> 00:15:36.780
I'd been trained to, or was used to doing,

00:15:37.340 --> 00:15:40.700
classic joinery detailing,

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:43.460
you know, tongued in, all the rest of it there.

00:15:45.280 --> 00:15:49.560
And Cedric's approach was a much more flexible one, in fact,

00:15:49.660 --> 00:15:50.940
sometimes too flexible.

00:15:52.500 --> 00:15:58.541
on the Docksport building, Frank Newby had quite rightly wanted to seal off the

00:15:58.541 --> 00:15:59.000
plenum

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:05.181
space in the roof with the air conditioning in block work because that gives you an

00:16:05.181 --> 00:16:06.000
impermeable

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:13.680
barrier. Cedric erupted at this because he hated the idea of what he conceived of as

00:16:13.680 --> 00:16:25.765
an all dry building having this nasty wet dirt blocks in it. So he suggested, well

00:16:25.765 --> 00:16:26.060
he

00:16:26.060 --> 00:16:34.060
required that, because by that time I got an assistant to work on the actual details

00:16:34.060 --> 00:16:42.248
through, the only time I ever had an assistant. He required that we do the cladding

00:16:42.248 --> 00:16:42.840
of the

00:16:42.840 --> 00:16:50.380
the plenum in pressed steel. Now that was a difficult one because you've got to make

00:16:50.380 --> 00:16:58.202
a plenum airtight for obvious reasons and he had an unwarranted faith in the

00:16:58.202 --> 00:16:59.540
subcontractor

00:16:59.540 --> 00:17:10.250
being able to produce an airtight gasket seal, which was not. I didn't want to do

00:17:10.250 --> 00:17:11.440
it, Frank

00:17:11.440 --> 00:17:15.810
didn't want to do it he insisted it would all be perfectly right he had not

00:17:15.810 --> 00:17:16.800
unwarranted faith

00:17:16.800 --> 00:17:22.600
it not just in technology but in other people's use for technology cedric's

00:17:22.600 --> 00:17:23.760
personality was

00:17:26.000 --> 00:17:32.240
immediate and fairly overwhelming to i think to most people who met him immensely

00:17:33.840 --> 00:17:39.975
generous and i mean he could he could talk to anybody and put anybody at their ease

00:17:39.975 --> 00:17:40.640
basically

00:17:41.440 --> 00:17:50.163
He was also supremely and rightly convinced of his own rightness, though he, as he

00:17:50.163 --> 00:17:51.440
always said,

00:17:51.440 --> 00:17:54.440
I'm always willing to admit I might be wrong.

00:17:54.440 --> 00:18:01.692
But I think on occasions when he actually admitted he was wrong, I can count on the

00:18:01.692 --> 00:18:03.440
fingers of one hand.

00:18:03.440 --> 00:18:09.482
But then on the other hand, he took care to make sure he was right, because he did

00:18:09.482 --> 00:18:10.440
his homework.

00:18:10.440 --> 00:18:18.547
He did the research, he did the delving and the digging to get to the right

00:18:18.547 --> 00:18:19.520
solution.

00:18:19.520 --> 00:18:27.800
A very attractive personality, particularly to women, of which he knew many.

00:18:27.800 --> 00:18:41.040
and the power to command an audience of as little as two or as many as a hundred

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:47.563
that that immense person that that's the thing that came through whenever he was

00:18:47.563 --> 00:18:48.460
speaking he

00:18:48.460 --> 00:18:54.147
was such an accomplished speaker the rhythm of his speech would draw the audience

00:18:54.147 --> 00:18:55.060
along with it

00:18:55.060 --> 00:19:00.402
It was only afterwards that you thought, hey, hang on a moment, am I sure about

00:19:00.402 --> 00:19:00.740
that?

00:19:01.640 --> 00:19:04.340
Why did he drink so much? Because he liked drinking.

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:08.560
We all drank too much at that time.

00:19:09.580 --> 00:19:11.480
I can't say I was abstemious.

00:19:13.920 --> 00:19:17.700
He, it kept him going.

00:19:18.560 --> 00:19:24.940
He had three things on his desk, a box of cigars,

00:19:25.060 --> 00:19:28.960
a bottle of brandy at a metronome.

00:19:31.420 --> 00:19:34.620
But he liked to drink.

00:19:35.680 --> 00:19:38.040
And at the back of his mind, I think,

00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:41.220
there was always the feeling that it might not last that long.

00:19:42.240 --> 00:19:48.320
Because, as you know, his father had a hereditary heart condition.

00:19:49.420 --> 00:19:51.740
And Cedric knew he'd inherited it.

00:19:51.740 --> 00:19:57.207
So there was always, I think, a bit of doubt in his mind about getting things done

00:19:57.207 --> 00:19:57.740
in time.

00:19:57.740 --> 00:20:05.554
Well, Cedric was a member of a large number of organisations, including some obscure

00:20:05.554 --> 00:20:13.182
ones like the Hot Stuff Club, the Confederation of British Demolition Contractors,

00:20:13.182 --> 00:20:17.740
all sorts of strange things which interested him.

00:20:17.740 --> 00:20:22.310
interested in him. Basically he became a member of things which he wanted to know a

00:20:22.310 --> 00:20:22.640
little

00:20:22.640 --> 00:20:27.274
bit more about. So if you're going to build a building and expect it to be

00:20:27.274 --> 00:20:27.900
demolished

00:20:27.900 --> 00:20:32.260
in 25 years you might as well make sure it can be done very easily.

00:20:32.260 --> 00:20:38.674
Hocksoff Club was a jolly get together drinking club. So there was David Alford and

00:20:38.674 --> 00:20:39.060
Frank

00:20:39.060 --> 00:20:45.299
Newby trying to think of the Herbert Gans, quite a lot of Americans, hard drinking

00:20:45.299 --> 00:20:46.060
Americans,

00:20:47.740 --> 00:21:01.722
Seoul, Kornberg. It was basically a sort of transatlantic club which included all

00:21:01.722 --> 00:21:02.240
the

00:21:02.240 --> 00:21:09.583
people that Cedric enjoyed talking with and exploring their minds and so forth. They

00:21:09.583 --> 00:21:10.020
would

00:21:10.020 --> 00:21:16.183
meet whenever one of them was in town basically, which was, you know, they were a

00:21:16.183 --> 00:21:17.020
peripatetic

00:21:17.460 --> 00:21:23.682
world around people. And they would meet up, it wasn't a fixed thing or anything

00:21:23.682 --> 00:21:24.460
like that,

00:21:25.760 --> 00:21:30.813
it was just, and it was really just a name for having a darn nice evening, lots of

00:21:30.813 --> 00:21:31.060
good

00:21:31.060 --> 00:21:35.687
food and good drink, you know. Well, since it's the only building I managed to put

00:21:35.687 --> 00:21:35.800
up

00:21:35.800 --> 00:21:41.655
for him, the Docksball building obviously because that was great fun. It was 12

00:21:41.655 --> 00:21:42.100
months

00:21:42.100 --> 00:21:48.774
from inception to completion. 12 months to design and build and get up a building.

00:21:48.774 --> 00:21:49.100
That

00:21:49.100 --> 00:21:55.387
was quite an interesting challenge. Housing research stemmed directly from one of

00:21:55.387 --> 00:21:55.620
the

00:21:55.620 --> 00:22:01.469
three housing types of the Thinkbelt, sprawl housing, which was originally designed

00:22:01.469 --> 00:22:01.680
for

00:22:01.680 --> 00:22:10.011
very uneven ground and very poor services or non-existent services. So the houses

00:22:10.011 --> 00:22:11.040
themselves

00:22:11.040 --> 00:22:17.360
were based on a triadic platform on jacks to accommodate for the ground because

00:22:17.360 --> 00:22:18.080
there was

00:22:18.080 --> 00:22:23.865
enormous subsidence going on all the time in the Thinkbelt. He walked into the

00:22:23.865 --> 00:22:25.200
office of what was

00:22:25.200 --> 00:22:30.295
called the regeneration department, not the planning department, and there was a big

00:22:30.295 --> 00:22:30.720
picture

00:22:30.720 --> 00:22:36.789
on the wall showing how much your house is likely to sink in the next five years

00:22:36.789 --> 00:22:37.320
because

00:22:37.320 --> 00:22:42.477
of all the mining substance. So that started off basically something you could plonk

00:22:42.477 --> 00:22:42.600
on

00:22:42.600 --> 00:22:51.281
ground, very lightweight, with timber frame on top. And to that we connected

00:22:51.281 --> 00:22:52.880
self-contained

00:22:52.880 --> 00:22:59.808
sewage digestion facilities which we got from the type of shipboard sewage digestion

00:22:59.808 --> 00:23:00.220
which

00:23:00.220 --> 00:23:07.720
were made by Anker at the time. And so the idea of decoupling surfaces, running them

00:23:07.720 --> 00:23:14.421
above ground, not having to dig out dirt and put in more dirt, you know, as Bucky

00:23:14.421 --> 00:23:15.000
put it,

00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:19.705
started there. So we then turned this into, at the same time we were working on,

00:23:19.705 --> 00:23:19.940
then

00:23:19.940 --> 00:23:25.536
we were working on the steel house competition. So that provided an impetus to look

00:23:25.536 --> 00:23:25.940
at the

00:23:25.940 --> 00:23:32.897
flexibility which a steel frame would give us. We were looking at the ability to

00:23:32.897 --> 00:23:33.680
customise

00:23:33.680 --> 00:23:44.255
the outside completely. Basically the housing as it developed had a prefabricated

00:23:44.255 --> 00:23:45.560
foundation

00:23:45.560 --> 00:23:53.167
raft which are now pretty commonplace today which weren't then. And then you sat the

00:23:53.167 --> 00:23:53.620
thing

00:23:53.620 --> 00:24:02.723
on top. That was steel frame, glazed, fully air-conditioned, and you know, you could

00:24:02.723 --> 00:24:02.940
do

00:24:02.940 --> 00:24:08.537
almost anything with it. And it lasted 25 years because that was the optimum

00:24:08.537 --> 00:24:09.200
financial

00:24:09.200 --> 00:24:13.378
investment time. You could use it after that of course, but you were basically

00:24:13.378 --> 00:24:13.860
recycling

00:24:13.860 --> 00:24:20.519
bits of it. That was linked to, in the sort of final studies I did, particularly on

00:24:20.519 --> 00:24:20.760
the

00:24:20.760 --> 00:24:28.683
Tilbury study. A concept of running all the services above ground under a boardwalk

00:24:28.683 --> 00:24:29.160
which

00:24:29.160 --> 00:24:37.760
would also support lighting and so forth. And the roadways were aluminium emergency

00:24:37.760 --> 00:24:45.275
runway strips, perforated aluminium. So your whole thing could be taken away after

00:24:45.275 --> 00:24:46.100
25 years.

00:24:46.100 --> 00:24:52.850
there was an artificial moon. An artificial moon to light it at night would go up

00:24:52.850 --> 00:24:53.100
and

00:24:53.480 --> 00:25:00.068
light the whole area. Which actually the way these things come round, many years

00:25:00.068 --> 00:25:00.480
later

00:25:03.980 --> 00:25:10.185
they were filming Sylvia, a film called Sylvia, about Sylvia Plath in our back

00:25:10.185 --> 00:25:10.980
garden and

00:25:10.980 --> 00:25:16.320
in our back garden and they were lighting a night scene and they had an inflatable

00:25:16.320 --> 00:25:16.580
moon

00:25:18.100 --> 00:25:25.178
oh god i've got a sentence to set you know it happened at last um so the housing

00:25:25.178 --> 00:25:25.620
study

00:25:25.620 --> 00:25:30.685
were basically looking at how you could add to existing um settlements without

00:25:30.685 --> 00:25:31.140
placing

00:25:31.140 --> 00:25:41.220
and demand on services and without sort of, well, investing as little as possible in

00:25:41.220 --> 00:25:41.940
things

00:25:41.940 --> 00:25:47.600
which would have to be left in the ground. Because the other thing we'd done is do a

00:25:47.600 --> 00:25:55.194
study into the GLC mobile homes, which were produced in the early 60s, too, because

00:25:55.194 --> 00:25:55.560
they

00:25:55.560 --> 00:25:59.850
had a lot of spare bomb sites and they had a big housing shortage and they produced

00:25:59.850 --> 00:26:00.160
rather

00:26:00.160 --> 00:26:05.500
good timber framed mobile homes so-called mobile they were supposed to

00:26:05.500 --> 00:26:11.140
be able to be moved from one site to another we actually followed one set of

00:26:11.140 --> 00:26:19.160
houses being moved from one site to another they were in sections and we

00:26:19.160 --> 00:26:24.880
were following one and the thing was bouncing along and you could see between

00:26:24.880 --> 00:26:32.680
the floor joists and bottle after gin bottle came rolling out of the from

00:26:32.680 --> 00:26:39.480
between the floor joists but the thing we learned from that was that the the

00:26:39.480 --> 00:26:48.660
amount of cost factor in services is quite disproportionate the amount of

00:26:48.660 --> 00:26:54.100
digging and laying and drainage and the rest of it made it really actually

00:26:54.100 --> 00:27:02.680
virtually it wasn't cost effective to move these things because you had to lay

00:27:02.680 --> 00:27:07.340
in this whole network of services if you're going to move them so that the

00:27:07.340 --> 00:27:12.720
nozzles Bucky put it on the top was was the least of your worries in terms of

00:27:12.720 --> 00:27:18.980
cost and I certainly ran into this later because once I after I'd left Cedric's

00:27:18.980 --> 00:27:23.140
office I think that's right I was asked to advise the Brixton Law Centre on

00:27:23.140 --> 00:27:25.140
on putting up a...

00:27:25.140 --> 00:27:29.560
They were servicing Brixton Jail.

00:27:31.240 --> 00:27:34.960
Doing a home for them, as it were.

00:27:35.240 --> 00:27:38.340
And I did investigate using GLC mobile homes,

00:27:38.420 --> 00:27:39.760
which were on the market at the time,

00:27:39.820 --> 00:27:42.720
but the same problem came up, cost of services.

00:27:43.460 --> 00:27:45.200
Site was easy, you could get a site,

00:27:45.760 --> 00:27:47.440
but the services were the problem.

00:27:47.840 --> 00:27:50.340
So the final bits of the housing study

00:27:50.340 --> 00:27:54.480
were trying to decouple from the big service networks,

00:27:54.920 --> 00:28:00.200
which effectively, we knew this because after the war,

00:28:01.280 --> 00:28:03.280
a hell of a lot of East London was rebuilt.

00:28:03.940 --> 00:28:09.240
But it was basically the sewers which determined the pattern of the new development,

00:28:09.760 --> 00:28:12.740
not anything else, because you couldn't do away with them

00:28:12.740 --> 00:28:15.460
because it would be far too expensive to change them.

00:28:15.460 --> 00:28:19.660
So your pattern of development was constrained by your services,

00:28:19.660 --> 00:28:21.100
which were underground.

00:28:21.960 --> 00:28:24.180
How did he manage to be so influential?

00:28:24.960 --> 00:28:29.760
By power of speech, by power of ideas, by power of drawings,

00:28:30.420 --> 00:28:35.120
by opening up possibilities which people hadn't thought of before.

00:28:35.240 --> 00:28:40.060
And remember, he had a track record very early on with the aviary.

00:28:41.060 --> 00:28:45.220
You know, he built it, which is no small thing.

00:28:45.220 --> 00:28:46.520
So he had credibility.

00:28:47.140 --> 00:28:49.520
He had architectural street cred, if you like.

00:28:49.660 --> 00:28:52.560
He wasn't just a dreamer or somebody.

00:28:52.720 --> 00:28:56.560
He was putting forward things which could be built

00:28:56.560 --> 00:28:59.400
and the reasons why they weren't built

00:28:59.400 --> 00:29:02.900
were very often nothing to do with him

00:29:02.900 --> 00:29:04.800
but with the fact that the client changed

00:29:04.800 --> 00:29:07.120
as with the Oxford Street Corner house

00:29:07.120 --> 00:29:10.880
where the Joe Lyons, the Salmon family changed its direction.

00:29:11.440 --> 00:29:14.620
So clients tend to follow Cedric around.

00:29:15.880 --> 00:29:19.340
Norman Payne, who was the chair of the British Airports Authority,

00:29:19.660 --> 00:29:23.317
then moved to British Transport, Docksport, which is why we built the commuter

00:29:23.317 --> 00:29:24.020
centre for him.

00:29:24.660 --> 00:29:29.620
Geoffrey Salmon of Joe Lyons moved to another section of Joe Lyons,

00:29:29.780 --> 00:29:36.700
and that's why the Blackpool restaurant is there, because that was their restaurant.

00:29:37.780 --> 00:29:43.971
So it wasn't the case so much of Cedric seeking out clients as clients seeking out

00:29:43.971 --> 00:29:44.500
Cedric.

00:29:45.000 --> 00:29:47.820
And one thought, coming back for more.

00:29:47.820 --> 00:29:52.860
Ah, well, Blackpool Zoo was another square building, by the way.

00:29:53.740 --> 00:29:54.600
Cedric liked squares.

00:29:55.840 --> 00:30:00.140
And this was Blackpool Zoo, and it was a restaurant for Blackpool Zoo.

00:30:00.460 --> 00:30:04.520
Now, Blackpool Zoo is part of the funfair.

00:30:04.680 --> 00:30:07.280
It's a sort of fairly curious place.

00:30:07.940 --> 00:30:13.280
And Cedric designed the restaurant so that if it didn't get off the ground,

00:30:13.280 --> 00:30:18.940
if it failed as a restaurant it could be converted into an animal house. The

00:30:18.940 --> 00:30:20.280
relevance is hope,

00:30:22.780 --> 00:30:29.530
confidence. This is a very unconfident era for architecture. Well it has been for

00:30:29.530 --> 00:30:29.780
the

00:30:29.780 --> 00:30:35.885
last twenty years basically. The relevance is also about having a much more

00:30:35.885 --> 00:30:36.780
fragmented,

00:30:36.780 --> 00:30:44.980
having a much more fragmented, flexible approach to the built environment, what we

00:30:44.980 --> 00:30:45.780
live in.

00:30:45.780 --> 00:30:52.918
That realising that it is never static, it's always changing, it's always renewing

00:30:52.918 --> 00:30:53.440
itself

00:30:53.440 --> 00:31:00.002
or being renewed, just as people are never static. And looking at built environment

00:31:00.002 --> 00:31:00.160
as

00:31:00.160 --> 00:31:05.574
a palimpsest of past people, which is something that very much interests me because

00:31:05.574 --> 00:31:05.900
I did

00:31:05.900 --> 00:31:11.480
specialize in quite a lot of historic building work. That's something you

00:31:11.480 --> 00:31:17.060
quickly realize you are dealing with. If you've got a 500 year old building and

00:31:17.060 --> 00:31:24.020
you can track what has happened to it, you are talking about a whole slew of

00:31:24.020 --> 00:31:29.900
generations of people who worked, lived and changed that building, you know, which

00:31:29.900 --> 00:31:35.240
is a very sobering thing. You're just one in that line and there will be

00:31:35.240 --> 00:31:43.080
others beyond you. Cedric was told that he had to accommodate a folly by Peter

00:31:43.080 --> 00:31:49.880
Smithson, Little Tower. All the drawings that Cedric produced after that showed

00:31:49.880 --> 00:31:54.640
this thing dotting up all over the place like that, changing where it

00:31:54.640 --> 00:32:05.720
was, whatever it was, you know. It became almost a dream set of drawings that just

00:32:05.720 --> 00:32:06.260
said

00:32:06.260 --> 00:32:10.970
this has appeared here but it could be there tomorrow. Look it's over there now and

00:32:10.970 --> 00:32:11.140
all

00:32:11.140 --> 00:32:20.093
the rest of it. And that was as his life progressed, I mean that was the change

00:32:20.093 --> 00:32:21.680
from, you know

00:32:21.680 --> 00:32:27.718
was initially with the Fun Palace he was designing buildings which could change. He

00:32:27.718 --> 00:32:28.300
was more

00:32:28.300 --> 00:32:34.734
interested in building as part of change by the end of his life. In other words, not

00:32:34.734 --> 00:32:35.040
that

00:32:35.040 --> 00:32:42.287
they would go anywhere, do anything, all that sort of stuff. That they would be part

00:32:42.287 --> 00:32:42.460
of

00:32:42.460 --> 00:32:48.720
a changing environment and adapt to it and adapt it. In other words, there should be

00:32:48.720 --> 00:32:55.939
an interactive environment between the built works, if you like, and the people who

00:32:55.939 --> 00:32:56.200
use

00:32:56.200 --> 00:33:03.239
and inhabit them. And that together would form a continuously changing pattern of

00:33:03.239 --> 00:33:03.760
living

00:33:03.760 --> 00:33:11.114
and learning. It's very difficult to put it into words. It's a feeling more of...

00:33:11.114 --> 00:33:11.840
Whenever

00:33:11.840 --> 00:33:16.913
I see one of Cedric's drawings, particularly the later ones, there is that feeling

00:33:16.913 --> 00:33:17.160
that

00:33:17.160 --> 00:33:23.666
comes out of it is that there's a mark being put on the city here, another mark

00:33:23.666 --> 00:33:24.160
there,

00:33:24.160 --> 00:33:29.160
and they change, you know, they become much more impermanent.

00:33:37.160 --> 00:33:43.827
Look at the projects, read the words, and then work out whether you like them or

00:33:43.827 --> 00:33:44.160
not.
